  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through six innings until the eighth broke it open.",
    "what_this_means": "The Padres didn't need to lead early. They needed to convert late, and that's exactly what happened. Ty France drove in three runs in a lineup that scratched out eleven hits and left four runners in scoring position. The bullpen was the real separator: JP Sears threw 3.1 scoreless with three strikeouts, Matsui added another clean inning, and Waldron finished the ninth with control intact. This wasn't a blowout or a comeback. It was a team that stayed in a tied game and capitalized when the door opened in the eighth. Jake Cronenworth's walk started that rally, but France's bat finished it. The pattern here isn't pretty. It's efficient. They don't blow teams away and they don't storm back from deficits. They hold serve and strike when the moment tightens. That's fragile math over a full season, but it's working right now. Two straight wins. Two straight nights of not folding when the game was still there to be won. France gave them three swings when they needed one.",
    "what_to_watch": "Miami's Janson Junk carries a 5.09 ERA into tonight's matchup. Walker Buehler takes the ball for San Diego at 10:40 AM PT.",
